<!--
  Plain HTTP is allowed, and this file exists so the reason is on the record rather than inferred
  from a one-word manifest attribute.

  Android has blocked cleartext by default since API 28, which is right for an app that talks to
  the internet. This one also has to talk to a model server on the home network, at an address its
  owner types in - `http://192.168.1.50:11434` - and Ollama serves plain HTTP with no option to do
  otherwise. Without this, every LAN address fails with "Cleartext HTTP traffic not permitted",
  which from the settings screen is indistinguishable from the server being switched off.

  This cannot be narrowed to the addresses that need it. A domain-config takes literal hostnames,
  and the whole point is that the address is unknown until the user supplies it - there is no
  wildcard for "the private address ranges".

  What is *not* loosened: certificate validation is untouched, so an https address is still
  checked against the system trust store, which is what a Cloudflare tunnel will use. Model
  downloads go to huggingface.co over https and stay there. The exposure this accepts is that a
  server address typed as http is readable on the local network - which is true of Ollama itself,
  and is the reason the settings screen asks for an auth header the moment the server is reachable
  from outside.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
